Since `TimestampFormatter` is a **static class**, you have two paths:

## Option A — Convert to a regular class (recommended)

This is the clean DI approach since `TimeProvider` is already registered:

```csharp
public class TimestampFormatter(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public string Format()
        => timeProvider.GetUtcNow().ToString("O");
}
```

Register it:
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<TimestampFormatter>();
```

Then inject `TimestampFormatter` where needed instead of calling it statically.

---

## Option B — Keep it static (ambient context)

If you can't change all call sites, use `AsyncLocal<T>`:

```csharp
public static class TimestampFormatter
{
    private static readonly AsyncLocal<TimeProvider?> s_override = new();

    public static TimeProvider Clock
    {
        get => s_override.Value ?? TimeProvider.System;
        set => s_override.Value = value;
    }

    public static string Format()
        => Clock.GetUtcNow().ToString("O");
}
```

Test by setting `TimestampFormatter.Clock = fakeTimeProvider;` — `AsyncLocal` keeps parallel tests isolated.

---

**My recommendation:** Go with **Option A**. Static classes with hidden dependencies are the testability problem — converting to a DI-friendly instance class is the whole point of the migration. Option B is a compromise for when refactoring callers isn't feasible yet.

For testing either approach, add:
```
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 6, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var formatter = new TimestampFormatter(fake); // Option A
Assert.Equal("2026-07-06T00:00:00.0000000+00:00", formatter.Format());
```

